  "worked_solution": "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/e2f13711ca62a8a4a698e23bdfe78caca6df44d88ded27b88c517219dffbb866.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-4,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,-3).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/dc7f93220ac18ac5671b7b41de141940ca5296139b836d56755fdccf6164ed26.png)",
